I do not know if this was "depleted" uranium (U238),
the stuff rejected in the enrichment process,
though.

That would not surprise me, it is safer yet, with
the nasty U235 bits mostly sucked out.

The cube was the property of a chemisty professor at
a summer enrichment class I attended at St. Cloud
State College, Minnesota, USA (as a rising HS
senior); he kept it on his desk as a paperweight,
normally, so it probably didn't tickle a geiger
counter at all, or he'd have had better sense.

The cube was a dark gray-black in color. It probably
had an oxide coating like aluminum acquires, I seem
to recall that metallic uranium is quite chemically
reactive, having a loose grip on its outer orbit
electrons, if so it probably oxidizes quickly in
air. I'm not sure what color untarnished uranium
might be, but silver always seems like a good first
guess for metals.



